{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792301683,"nonce":"5vOIwtZvN1heVDFG5lXJA6zUgsM=","title":"A frame whose glyphs overflow the atlases twice clears them and draws no text","message":"**What**\n\nhl_blinc_tree_display_list walks a frame twice at most: if its glyphs overflow the glyph atlases it starts them afresh and walks again. It cleared them after every overflowing walk, including the second, and then returned that walk's records. Those records name the glyphs that did fit, at places in atlases that were now empty, so the caller uploaded an empty atlas and every glyph of the frame drew as nothing, text everywhere. The second walk's polygon points were also dropped, so a polygon record read the wrong rows.\n\nSeen in ashui (its git-bug c2cfa7920f3b98702970248895f35d2fd48438f5612c576d22fb75b7282f505c): glyphs of a font with CFF outlines rasterized in font units, a thousand pixels a side, filled the 4096 by 4096 atlas.\n\n**Fix**\n\nClear between the walks only. The second walk's records and points stand even when it overflows too: what did not fit is left out and the rest draws. The rule is a function, build_with_retry, with tests for a frame that fits, one that overflows once and one that overflows twice.\n\n**Not this**\n\nThe CFF scaling that fills the atlas is in blinc_text's rasterizer (the Blinc repository), asked of its owner. scene.rs's SceneEncoder::prepare has the same two-walk loop but returns an error after clearing, so its caller knows; left as it is.","files":null}]}